<TopicPage slug="subjects/nuclear-engineering/foundational/radiation-physics-and-detection">

  <h2>Where you are, and what you will learn</h2>
  <p>
    This is the first lesson in Nuclear Engineering, and it needs almost nothing from other
    lessons — only everyday arithmetic (halving, percentages) and a willingness to think in
    very large numbers. The one piece of atomic vocabulary you need — that an atom has a tiny
    nucleus of protons and neutrons around which electrons sit — is rebuilt here. Everything
    else in the discipline is this page applied.
  </p>
  <p>By the end you will be able to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>explain why nuclei decay, and name the decay modes and what each one emits;</li>
    <li>use the exponential decay law and the half-life, and compute how much activity survives a given time;</li>
    <li>predict how alpha, beta, gamma and neutron radiation each interact with matter, and how each is shielded;</li>
    <li>describe how a Geiger tube, a scintillator and a semiconductor detector turn invisible events into numbers — and what information each one keeps and throws away;</li>
    <li>say why every measurement of radiation is a statistical estimate, and how honest error bars are attached.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    One habit: whenever anyone states a number about radiation, ask three questions —
    <em>how many events, over what time, measured with what</em>. Radiation is invisible and
    unfelt, so in this discipline the instrument is not an accessory; it is the only witness.
  </p>

  <h2>Radiation you cannot see, hear or feel</h2>
  <p>
    In 1896 the French physicist Henri Becquerel left some uranium salts in a drawer on top of
    a wrapped photographic plate, and found the plate fogged where the salts had sat. Nothing
    he could see, hear, smell or touch had passed between them — yet something had, and it
    had altered chemistry through paper. That accident is the field's founding observation,
    and it contains its central difficulty: ionising radiation affects the body but triggers
    no nerve impulses. Victims of severe radiation exposure feel nothing at the moment of
    exposure; burns and blood-count changes arrive days later.
  </p>
  <p>
    This is why detection by instruments is more than a research tool here. Because no sense
    reaches the hazard, everything — safety, medicine, safeguards, reactor operation — runs
    through machines that convert invisible events into counts, currents and spectra. The
    plan of this lesson follows that chain: why unstable nuclei decay, what they emit, what
    the emissions do to matter, and how matter is turned into measurement.
  </p>

  <h2>Why nuclei decay at all: a tug-of-war</h2>
  <p>
    The nucleus is a tug-of-war. The strong nuclear force binds protons and neutrons
    together, while the electrical repulsion between the positively charged protons pushes
    them apart. For light elements, stability favours roughly equal numbers of each (carbon-12:
    six and six); as elements get heavier, extra neutrons are needed as nuclear "glue", and
    past bismuth-209 no configuration is stable at all. When a nucleus has the wrong
    neutron-to-proton ratio — or simply too much mass — it carries excess energy, and nature
    sheds that energy by emitting particles and radiation.
  </p>
  <p>
    Individual decays are utterly random: no physics can tell you when one particular atom
    will decay. Yet the <em>statistics</em> of trillions of atoms are the most precise laws
    in engineering. Learning to think in ensembles rather than atoms is the discipline's
    first intellectual leap, and the widget below sketches the whole journey you are about to
    take — decay, penetration, timing, detection and dose.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="decay, penetration, detection — and dose" widget="equationStep" config={radiationCfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> the five cards run from cause to consequence. Card 1 is
    the microscopic event; card 2 is why the same event harms differently depending on what
    was emitted; card 3 is the timing law that organises everything from medical isotopes to
    waste policy; cards 4 and 5 are the human interface — how the events get counted, and why
    the count alone never settles the safety question. Keep the chain in view: this page is
    one story, not five topics.
  </p>

  <h2>The decay modes: what the nucleus emits</h2>
  <p>
    The decay modes sort by what the nucleus is trying to fix. <strong>Alpha decay</strong>
    ejects a helium-4 nucleus (two protons, two neutrons, a very tightly bound package) — the
    route taken by heavy nuclei such as uranium-238 and radium-226, which reduce both their
    mass and their proton count in one move. <strong>Beta-minus decay</strong> fixes a
    neutron excess: a neutron converts to a proton, emitting an electron and an antineutrino
    (carbon-14 becoming nitrogen-14 is the famous case, and the basis of radiocarbon dating).
    <strong>Beta-plus decay</strong> and <strong>electron capture</strong> fix the opposite
    problem — a proton excess — by converting a proton to a neutron (fluorine-18 decaying by
    positron emission is the engine of PET imaging). <strong>Gamma emission</strong> usually
    follows the others: the daughter nucleus is born in an excited state and sheds pure
    electromagnetic energy — no particle, no change of identity, just photons. When a nucleus
    is extremely neutron-rich it can even emit a neutron outright, which is why fission
    products chain into the delayed-neutron physics you will meet later in reactor theory.
  </p>

  <h2>The law that never blinks: half-life and activity</h2>
  <p>
    The timing of all this is governed by one law. Decay is a constant-probability event:
    each nucleus of a given species has a fixed chance λ of decaying per unit time,
    independent of its age, temperature, pressure or chemistry. For a population N that gives
    dN/dt = −λN, and hence the exponential N = N<sub>0</sub>e<sup>−λt</sup> — whose friendly
    face is the <strong>half-life</strong> (t<sub>½</sub> = 0.693/λ), the time for half of any
    sample to decay. <strong>Activity</strong> — decays per second, measured in becquerels
    (Bq; the older curie equals 3.7 × 10<sup>10</sup> Bq) — follows the same curve, since
    A = λN.
  </p>

  <Widget no="2" caption="three half-lives, three timelines" widget="plotter" config={decayCfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> the three curves are the same law wearing three
    personalities. Iodine-131 (8 days) is intensely radioactive and briefly so — ideal for
    treating thyroid tumours and gone within months. Caesium-137 (30 years) decays slowly
    enough to define the decades-long contamination legacy of Chernobyl and Fukushima. The
    72-second nuclide is effectively gone before you can measure it twice. Now read the
    arithmetic off the graph: hold a source of iodine-131 for 32 days and it has passed four
    half-lives, so 1/2<sup>4</sup> = 1/16 — about 6 per cent — of the initial activity
    remains; the same wait applied to caesium-137 removes almost nothing. Multiply these
    curves by the thousands of nuclides in spent nuclear fuel and you have <strong>decay
    heat</strong> — the reason spent fuel sits in cooling pools for years and dry casks for
    decades. The entire waste-timeline debate is exponentials arguing with each other.
  </p>
  <p>
    Heavy nuclides rarely decay in one step. Uranium-238 heads a <strong>decay chain</strong>:
    fourteen successive emissions — eight alphas and six betas — through radium-226 and the
    gas radon-222 down to stable lead-206. Chains create subtleties that matter later: a
    daughter's activity can grow while the parent decays, and in <strong>secular
    equilibrium</strong> (parent half-life vastly longer) the chain members all decay at the
    parent's rate — which is why old uranium ore is a cocktail of the whole chain, and why
    radon, a gas that escapes rock into basements, is a decay-chain link with public-health
    weight.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> that "radioactive" describes a substance, full stop — so a
  more radioactive thing is automatically more dangerous. Radioactivity is an event rate, not
  a substance property, and hazard depends on what is emitted, at what energy, and where the
  source sits relative to the body. A curie of tritium (a weak beta emitter) is a lab
  curiosity; a curie of caesium-137 (a strong gamma emitter) is a severe incident. The number
  on the Geiger counter is the beginning of the safety analysis, not its conclusion.</p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> that we could make waste harmless by heating it, freezing
  it or dissolving it. The decay constant λ is fixed by nuclear structure — the same physics
  that makes it independent of temperature, pressure and chemical bonds. Chemistry rearranges
  electrons; radioactivity happens in the nucleus, a hundred thousand times deeper in. That
  is exactly why decay heat cannot be switched off and why storage, not treatment, is the
  engineering answer: you cannot hurry an exponential.</p>

  <h2>How radiation meets matter</h2>
  <p>
    Radiation is classified by what it is, and the classification dictates both harm and
    shielding. <strong>Charged particles</strong> lose energy continuously through electrical
    interactions with atomic electrons, ionising as they go. <strong>Alphas</strong> are heavy
    (about 7,300 electron masses) and doubly charged, so they ionise ferociously but brake
    fast: a 5 MeV alpha travels a few centimetres in air and is stopped by a sheet of paper —
    or by the dead outer layer of skin. <strong>Betas</strong> are light and fast, travelling
    metres in air and millimetres to centimetres in tissue, stopped by a few millimetres of
    plastic; their one shielding subtlety is that braking in dense, high-Z material (lead)
    produces secondary X-rays (bremsstrahlung), so beta shields are plastic or aluminium,
    with lead only behind them if gammas follow. Engineers capture the alpha-versus-beta
    contrast in the term LET (linear energy transfer): how much energy is deposited per unit
    path length.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Gamma rays and X-rays</strong> — photons, uncharged — interact in three ways whose
    balance depends on energy and material. At low energies the <strong>photoelectric
    effect</strong> dominates: the photon is absorbed wholesale, ejecting an electron (scaling
    steeply with atomic number — the physics behind both lead shielding and medical X-ray
    contrast). At middle energies, <strong>Compton scattering</strong>: the photon bounces off
    an electron, losing part of its energy and changing direction — the main hazard mechanism
    in shielding design, since scattered photons continue as a degraded beam. Above 1.022 MeV,
    <strong>pair production</strong>: the photon converts into an electron and a positron in
    the nuclear field. Because all three remove photons stochastically from a beam, gamma
    attenuation is exponential: I = I<sub>0</sub>e<sup>−μx</sup>, with each half-value layer of
    material halving the intensity. <strong>Neutrons</strong>, being uncharged, ignore
    electron clouds and interact only with nuclei: they scatter (best off nuclei of similar
    mass — hydrogen, hence water, plastic and concrete as moderators), get captured (boron-10
    and cadmium are legendary absorbers), and can activate stable materials into radioactive
    ones — the reason the steel of a reactor vessel remains radioactive long after the
    reactor stops. Shielding a neutron source is therefore a sandwich: hydrogenous material to
    slow the neutrons, boron to absorb them, and lead or concrete behind to catch the capture
    gammas.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "alpha is the harmless one — paper stops it." Paper stops
  it from <em>outside</em>. Inside the body — inhaled radon daughters, ingested polonium-210
  — an alpha source deposits all its energy within a few microns of living tissue, no dead
  skin layer in the way: the most locally damaging radiation there is. The same physics that
  makes alpha safe at arm's length makes it the worst-case internal hazard, which is why
  protection rules care so much about preventing intake.</p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "with enough lead, gamma radiation is stopped
  completely." Exponential attenuation never reaches zero — each half-value layer halves what
  is left, and a half of a half of a half is never nothing. That is why shielding is
  specified in half-value layers (a hundred HVLs, a factor of 10<sup>30</sup> reduction, is a
  real design number), and why "safe" in this business always means "reduced to an accepted
  fraction", never "zero".</p>

  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> unstable nuclei repair themselves by emitting alpha, beta or
    gamma radiation on a fixed exponential timetable; the emissions meet matter by ionising,
    scattering or being absorbed, and the meeting is what both the hazard and the detection
    consist of. One bridge remains: from an ionisation inside a chunk of matter to a number
    on a display.
  </p>

  <h2>Detection: turning ionisations into numbers</h2>
  <p>
    Every detector exploits the same fact: radiation ionises and excites matter, and those
    events can be counted. The oldest method is Becquerel's own: <strong>film</strong>. A
    quantum of energy alters the emulsion chemically, exactly as light does in a dental
    X-ray, and the darkening is related to exposure — though it also depends on the type of
    radiation, which is why personal film badges sandwich the film between different
    absorbers so that the pattern of darkening reveals what penetrated.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Gas-filled detectors</strong> differ by the voltage across the chamber. A
    Geiger–Müller tube is a conducting cylinder with a thin wire along its axis, filled with
    insulating gas; the voltage between cylinder and wire produces almost no current until
    radiation passing through creates free ion pairs, which are swept to the electrodes as a
    pulse of current — one <em>count</em>. The word count is precise: a simple Geiger counter
    gives no information on energy, charge or type of radiation, and misses particles that
    pass through without enough ionisation. What it gives, cheaply and sturdily, is a prompt
    reading of the existence and relative intensity of radiation — the clicking of every
    survey meter. An <em>ionisation chamber</em> runs at lower voltage and collects the
    primary ion pairs directly, giving a current proportional to dose — the instrument of
    dose-rate surveys; a <em>proportional counter</em> runs in between, each ion pair seeding
    a small avalanche that preserves energy information.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Scintillators</strong> take the other route: materials such as NaI(Tl) emit
    flashes of light when radiation deposits energy in them — liquid or solid, very
    efficient, and fast enough to count enormous event rates. A <strong>photomultiplier</strong>
    converts each flash into an electrical cascade: the light ejects an electron from a metal
    plate (the photoelectric effect), the electron is accelerated to the next plate where it
    knocks out two or more, and so on through stages — the final pulse remaining proportional
    to the light, and so to the energy deposited. That is gamma spectroscopy, which
    identifies nuclides by their photon signatures (the 662 keV line of caesium-137 is the
    standard's standard). <strong>Semiconductor detectors</strong> (high-purity germanium)
    convert ionisation directly into a signal, with the deposited energy closely readable
    from the pulse and high efficiency — resolving gamma lines that NaI only smears together
    — at the price of cryogenic cooling. <strong>Neutrons</strong>, invisible to all of the
    above, demand converters: boron-trifluoride or helium-3 tubes counting the charged
    fragments of the neutron-boron reaction, fission chambers, or hydrogen-rich scintillators
    reading recoil protons. Personal dosimetry completes the picture — thermoluminescent and
    optically stimulated dosimeters accumulating dose like exposure film, read monthly.
  </p>
  <p>
    Because emission is random, every measurement is a sample from a Poisson distribution,
    and the statistical uncertainty of a count N is approximately √N. Count 100 events and
    the uncertainty is ±10 per cent; count 10,000 and it is ±1 per cent. A decade of lab
    students discovering that "my count rate disagrees with theory" has rediscovered this —
    the answer is usually not a broken instrument but an under-counted average. Competent
    nuclear measurement therefore always reports the error bars, and competent reactor
    operators, health physicists and safeguards inspectors all live by the same discipline:
    radiation detection is statistics with instrumentation, never certainty with a needle.
  </p>

  <h2>Check yourself</h2>
  <p>Try these before moving on. Feedback follows each answer.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>A beta-minus decay fixes what problem in a nucleus — and what is emitted?</li>
    <li>Iodine-131 has a half-life of 8 days. A hospital holds a source for 24 days. Roughly what fraction of the original activity remains?</li>
    <li>Why are beta shields made of plastic rather than lead?</li>
    <li>A Geiger counter shows 100 counts in one minute. A colleague says the true rate is exactly 100 per minute. What is wrong with that claim?</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    <strong>Feedback:</strong> (1) A neutron excess: a neutron converts to a proton, emitting
    an electron and an antineutrino. (2) Three half-lives have passed, so (1/2)³ = 1/8 —
    about 12 per cent. If you divided by 3 rather than halving three times, remember the law
    is exponential, not linear. (3) High-Z braking produces bremsstrahlung — secondary X-rays
    — so lead converts the beta problem into a photon problem; low-Z plastic stops betas
    without manufacturing new radiation. (4) With N = 100 counts, the statistical uncertainty
    is √N = 10 — the rate is 100 ± 10 per minute at best, before any instrument error. More
    counting, not a better needle, narrows the band.
  </p>

  <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Nuclei decay when they have the wrong neutron-to-proton ratio or too much mass; alpha, beta, positron/electron-capture, gamma and neutron emission are the repair routes.</li>
    <li>Decay is constant-probability: N = N<sub>0</sub>e<sup>−λt</sup>, activity halves every half-life, and nothing physical can change the rate.</li>
    <li>Activity (Bq) is an event rate, not a danger dial — hazard depends on emission type, energy and location of the source.</li>
    <li>Charged particles ionise along their track (alphas ferociously, briefly — deadly inside); photons attenuate exponentially by half-value layers; neutrons need slowing, then absorbing.</li>
    <li>Detectors count ionisations: film records penetration history, gas tubes count events, scintillators and semiconductors preserve energy, neutron detection needs converters.</li>
    <li>Every count is statistical: uncertainty ≈ √N, so honest measurement reports error bars.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where this goes next</h2>
  <p>
    This page is the physics backbone for everything the discipline does later: reactor
    criticality builds on decay and neutron interactions, shielding design on the attenuation
    law, waste policy on the exponential timeline. Across the atlas, chemistry's lesson on
    atomic structure supplies the electron-level picture this page deliberately skipped, and
    the nuclear-medicine lesson on radiopharmaceuticals shows the same decays deliberately
    implanted in patients — fluorine-18, iodine-131 and their kin put to work. Wherever you go
    next, the ensemble habit travels with you: no single atom can be predicted, but trillions
    of them are the most reliable machinery in engineering.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    Three deeper threads show where the foundation leads. <strong>Decay heat</strong>: a
    shutdown reactor does not switch off — the decaying fission products continue to generate
    roughly 6–7 per cent of full power immediately after scram, falling as the short-lived
    nuclides exhaust themselves. This single consequence of the exponential law sized the
    emergency cooling systems of every reactor and drove the Fukushima accident's
    core-isolation dynamics; the accident's ultimate lesson was about decay heat and backup
    power, not criticality. <strong>Activity versus dose</strong>: connecting becquerels to
    sieverts runs through emission energy, decay branching, interaction physics, biological
    weighting and geometry — the chain of conversion factors that health physics as a
    profession maintains, with the ICRP's linear-no-threshold assumption for stochastic risk
    remaining the field's most consequential and most debated scientific commitment.
    <strong>Detection limits and metrology</strong>: real measurements face backgrounds
    (cosmic rays, radon daughters, the detector's own noise), so a "detection" is a
    statistical decision with a minimum detectable activity — the arithmetic behind
    safeguards declarations and environmental surveys alike — and every survey meter's
    credibility descends from national standards laboratories through calibrated sources in
    an unbroken chain of traceability.
  </p>
  <p>
    Applications radiate outward from the same physics: carbon-14 dating (a 5,730-year
    half-life used as a decay clock), PET imaging (positron annihilation's two 511 keV gammas
    flying in opposite directions), neutron activation analysis identifying trace elements,
    and the americium-241 ionisation chamber in an ordinary smoke detector. The discipline's
    promise to a student is unusual: learn one curve and three interactions, and you can read
    reactor safety cases, hospital physics, environmental monitoring and non-proliferation —
    they are all this page, wearing different overalls.
  </p>

